                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 862,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 1116,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Gives the spectrum of a whole signal - how much power there is at each frequency - by cutting it into overlapping windows, taking the spectrum of each and averaging them. Welch's method.\n\nA single spectrum of a long, noisy recording is ragged: every bin jumps about. Averaging many windows gives a steady, readable answer, at the price of coarser frequency steps. Use it to find the main rhythms in a take - a gait's step rate, a breathing rate, a tremor's frequency - or to compare how much fast and slow movement two performances have.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.welch <window: int> <sample rate>\nDefaults: window 64, sample rate 60.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nscipy.welch 256 100\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe signal: a list or 1-D array. Every input is analysed and sent.\n\nsample rate (float box, also an inlet):\nSamples per second, so the frequencies come out in cycles per second.\n\nwindow (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many samples in each window. Longer gives finer frequency steps (sample rate / window apart) but fewer windows to average.\n\noverlap (float box, also an inlet):\nHow much each window overlaps the last, as a fraction (0.5 by default).\n\ndecibels:\nAn option, under the node. Off (the default here), plain power; on, in decibels.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\npower:\nThe power at each frequency.\n\nfrequencies:\nThe frequency of each value, from 0 up to half the sample rate. Sent first.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe make_two_tones subpatcher makes 10 seconds of a wave at 3 cycles a second plus one half as tall at 8. Welch's spectrum, in windows of 128 (bins 0.47 cycles a second apart), has two peaks: near 3, and near 8 - a quarter as tall, since power goes with the square of the size.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.spectrogram keeps the windows apart, to show change over time. np.fft.rfft gives the spectrum of a single window. spectrum analyses a live stream.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 1100,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Gives the spectrum of a whole signal - how much power there is at each frequency - by cutting it into overlapping windows, taking the spectrum of each and averaging them. Welch's method.\n\nA single spectrum of a long, noisy recording is ragged: every bin jumps about. Averaging many windows gives a steady, readable answer, at the price of coarser frequency steps. Use it to find the main rhythms in a take - a gait's step rate, a breathing rate, a tremor's frequency - or to compare how much fast and slow movement two performances have.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.welch <window: int> <sample rate>\nDefaults: window 64, sample rate 60.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nscipy.welch 256 100\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe signal: a list or 1-D array. Every input is analysed and sent.\n\nsample rate (float box, also an inlet):\nSamples per second, so the frequencies come out in cycles per second.\n\nwindow (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many samples in each window. Longer gives finer frequency steps (sample rate / window apart) but fewer windows to average.\n\noverlap (float box, also an inlet):\nHow much each window overlaps the last, as a fraction (0.5 by default).\n\ndecibels:\nAn option, under the node. Off (the default here), plain power; on, in decibels.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\npower:\nThe power at each frequency.\n\nfrequencies:\nThe frequency of each value, from 0 up to half the sample rate. Sent first.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe make_two_tones subpatcher makes 10 seconds of a wave at 3 cycles a second plus one half as tall at 8. Welch's spectrum, in windows of 128 (bins 0.47 cycles a second apart), has two peaks: near 3, and near 8 - a quarter as tall, since power goes with the square of the size.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.spectrogram keeps the windows apart, to show change over time. np.fft.rfft gives the spectrum of a single window. spectrum analyses a live stream."
                },
